xisting technologies into a cohesive system around 1440. His crucial innovations included:\n\n- **Movable type**: Individual letters cast from a lead-tin alloy that could be arranged, inked, and reused. This drew on earlier experiments with movable type in China and Korea, but Gutenberg's version was more practical for European languages.\n- **The printing press**: Adapted from the screw presses used in wine and oil production, this allowed for consistent pressure and higher quality impressions.\n- **Oil-based ink**: More suitable for adhering to paper than the water-based inks used for manuscripts.\n- **Paper manufacturing**: Widely available paper, having spread from the Islamic world through Spain and Italy, provided an affordable substrate.\n\nThe result was the Gutenberg Bible,"}